  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nLight lake effect snow showers taper off this morning with drier \nconditions settling back into the region. Temperatures will be \nmuch cooler today with northwest flow in place, and 850 mb \ntemperatures dipping near -10 degrees C. As a result highs will \nrange in the mid 30s to low 40s. Winds will be relatively gusty \ntoday up to 15-20 knots behind the front. Otherwise clear skies \nsettle back in once lake effect clouds dissipate. Clear \nconditions are expected to follow into the evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nConfidence remains high in a widespread rain event later this week\nas an area of low pressure develops near the Southeast Coast \nand lifts northward along the Appalachians. The track of this \nsystem favors a strong influx of warm moisture rich air into the\nnortheast. Southeasterly flow strengthens Wednesday night \nallowing 850 mb temperatures to climb toward 10 degrees C ahead \nof the arriving precipitation. With warm air in place this \nlooks to be an all rain event. \n\nThere is also potential for strong winds with this system. A \npronounced low level jet will accompany the incoming precipitation, \nwith forecast soundings depicting winds of 60 to 80 mph just 3,000 \nfeet above ground level Thursday afternoon. Despite the \nimpressive winds aloft a stable boundary layer should prevent \nmuch of this momentum from mixing down to the surface. BUFKIT \nprofiles continue to favor gusts in the 20 to 30 mph range, \nkeeping most locations below wind advisory criteria. However, \nstronger gusts cannot be ruled out across the highest elevations\nof the Caskills and the Pocono Mountain region. Wind headlines \nmay be needed in the near future. \n\nAs the upper low passes through New York Friday into Saturday, it \nbegins to occlude and becomes stacked. The rain will become more \nshowery in nature with winds decreasing significantly. Model trends \ncontinue with less cold air wrapping into the occluding low from the \nGreat Lakes trough so even after cold air advection begins, \nprecipitation likely does not change over to snow. The exception may \nbe higher elevations in Steuben or the Catskills above 2000 feet.",
